# One wire running backwards, which is a lowpass built by hand out of an add and a
# multiply, with the one number that is its cutoff swept so it can be heard to be
# one.

description "A wire running backwards: a lowpass built from an add and a multiply, with"
  "its one number swept."
tags "basics" "effects"

# How much of the last evaluation is kept, which is the whole of the filter: the
# nearer one, the lower the cutoff. The useful range is all in the last tenth.
let keep = sine(freq: 0.1) |> remap(-1..1, 0.92..0.996)

# A square, because its corners are what a lowpass takes off. What is let in is
# what is not kept, so the loop comes out as loud as it went in.
let sum  = square(freq: 110) * (1 - keep) |> add(a: _)

# The wire that closes the loop, carrying the evaluation before: what makes this a
# filter rather than a ring of wires with nothing in it.
sum.b <- sum * keep
sum |> out.left

out.volume = 0.2
